<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suq al-Shuyukh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Souq al-Shuyukh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suq al-Shuyukh is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in Dhi Qar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, on the edge of the marshes. Its name means 'market of the sheikhs': it grew in the 18th century as the market town and base of the Sa'dun chiefs of the Muntafiq confederation, trading in dates, grain and livestock.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suq-al-shuyukh.jpg" alt="Suq al-Shuyukh" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS067-E-169910_-_View_of_Earth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suq al-Shuyukh stands among date palm orchards on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, at the western end of the Hawr al-Hammar lake and wetlands, about 40 km southeast of Nasiriya. The town is the centre of a district of the Dhi Qar Governorate, and the land to the north and west of the lake is given over to the cultivation of dates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was founded in the early 18th century in Ottoman Iraq to serve as the marketplace of the Muntafiq tribal confederation, and the word Shuyukh in its name refers to the members of the clan of the confederation's leader. Toward the end of the 18th century it was described as a small town with a mosque, surrounded by earthen walls. Accounts of the early 1800s put its people at 6,000 families and describe it as a centre of trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, and even with Bushir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>.</p><p>In the 19th century the town was divided into Muslim, Christian, Jewish and Mandaean quarters, as Julius Heinrich Petermann reported after his visit of 1854. The Mandaean quarter, known as Margab or Ṣubbūye, lay on the opposite bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Shortly before his visit, around 1853, about 200 of the town's roughly 260 Mandaean families, many of them silversmiths or boat-builders, had moved to al-Amarah because of persecution by the Muntafiq. Petermann estimated the population at 3,000 and met Yahya Bihram, the high priest of the Mandaeans.</p><p>Around the end of the 19th century V. Cuinet put the population at about 12,000: some 2,250 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> with two mosques, 8,770 Shi'is with one sanctuary, 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and 700 Mandaeans. Under Ottoman rule Suq al-Shuyukh became the seat of a kaza in the sanjak of Muntafiq. The town later took part in the Iraqi revolt of 1920 and in the Iraqi Shia revolts of 1935 and 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suq al-Shuyukh remains a centre of date and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> growing on the edge of the marshes. It has a stadium, the Suq Al-Shuyukh Stadium, and two football clubs, Suq Al-Shuyukh FC and Al-Forat FC.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suq al-Shuyukh District” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thuwayni ibn Abdullah was a dominant figure in the late 18th century, and the town of <b>Suq al-Shuyukh</b> served as their market and base.</blockquote><a href="/state/muntafiq-confederation">Muntafiq confederation</a></li></ul></section>
